Based on the screenshot, the application team is requesting two permissions for the Elasticsearch API key you already provided:

1. Cluster health monitoring: Add the `monitor` cluster privilege to allow calls such as `GET /_cluster/health`.
2. Index read access: Allow reading `logs-kubernetes.container_logs-openshift`.

The existing API key has `"cluster": []`, which explains the cluster health authorization failure. It already has `"names": ["*"]` and `"privileges": ["read"]`, so the requested index should already be covered, assuming the API key owner has that access.

## 1. Updated API key permissions (recommended)

For least-privilege access, use:

```

{
  "read-only-role": {
    "cluster": [
      "monitor"
    ],
    "indices": [
      {
        "names": [
          "logs-kubernetes.container_logs-openshift"
        ],
        "privileges": [
          "read"
        ],
        "allow_restricted_indices": false
      }
    ],
    "applications": [],
    "run_as": [],
    "metadata": {},
    "transient_metadata": {
      "enabled": true
    }
  }
}

```

If the team still requires access to all non-restricted indices, keep `"names": ["*"]` instead. In that case, the only necessary change to the permissions shown in your screenshot is `"cluster": ["monitor"]`.

The `monitor` privilege is sufficient for cluster health and is read-only, although it also permits other cluster monitoring operations.&#x20;

